# Security Rules (Customer-Facing Products)

## OWASP Top 10 Awareness (2025)

### A01: Broken Access Control
- Implement access control checks on EVERY endpoint — not just the UI
- Deny by default — explicitly grant permissions, don't assume access
- Validate that the authenticated user has permission to access the requested resource
- Don't rely on client-side access control — enforce on the server
- Log all access control failures

### A02: Cryptographic Failures
- Use TLS (HTTPS) for all data in transit
- Encrypt sensitive data at rest
- Use strong, current algorithms (AES-256, bcrypt/argon2 for passwords)
- Don't implement your own cryptography — use established libraries
- Rotate secrets and keys on a schedule

### A03: Injection & Supply Chain
- Use parameterized queries for ALL database operations
- Use ORM methods instead of raw SQL where possible
- Validate and sanitize all input — type, length, range, format
- Use allowlists over blocklists for input validation
- Escape output based on context (HTML, URL, SQL, OS command)
- Pin all dependency versions with lockfiles
- Audit dependencies for known vulnerabilities regularly
- Verify package names to prevent typosquatting attacks

### A05: Security Misconfiguration
- Remove default credentials and accounts
- Disable directory listing
- Set secure HTTP headers (Content-Security-Policy, X-Content-Type-Options, etc.)
- Don't expose stack traces or debug info in production errors
- Keep dependencies updated — check for known vulnerabilities
- Validate configuration at startup — fail fast on missing required values

### A07: Cross-Site Scripting (XSS)
- Encode output based on the context (HTML body, attribute, JavaScript, CSS, URL)
- Use Content Security Policy headers
- Sanitize HTML content from users with a battle-tested library (DOMPurify, Bleach)
- Avoid `innerHTML`, `dangerouslySetInnerHTML`, `eval()` with user content

## Input Validation
- Validate ALL inputs on the server side — client validation is for UX only
- Check: type, length, range, format, allowed characters
- Reject unexpected fields in request bodies
- Use a validation library (Pydantic, FluentValidation, Zod, Joi)
- Return specific, helpful validation error messages
- Validate file uploads: type, size, content (not just extension)

## Authentication
- Use established auth libraries — don't build your own
- Enforce strong password requirements or use passwordless auth
- Implement account lockout after failed attempts (with progressive delays)
- Use secure session management:
  - HttpOnly, Secure, SameSite flags on cookies
  - Regenerate session ID after login
  - Set appropriate session timeouts
- Multi-factor authentication for admin/sensitive operations
- Never store passwords in plain text — use bcrypt or argon2

## Authorization
- Implement role-based (RBAC) or attribute-based (ABAC) access control
- Check authorization at the API/service layer, not just the UI
- Log authorization failures for monitoring
- Implement principle of least privilege — users get minimum required access
- Separate admin and user functionality
- Prevent IDOR (Insecure Direct Object Reference) — validate ownership on every resource access

## Rate Limiting
- Implement rate limiting on all authentication endpoints
- Rate limit API endpoints to prevent abuse
- Use progressive delays for repeated failures (exponential backoff)
- Return 429 Too Many Requests with Retry-After header
- Monitor for rate limit bypass attempts
- Consider different limits for authenticated vs unauthenticated users

## CORS Configuration
- Don't use `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` in production
- Whitelist specific allowed origins
- Restrict allowed methods and headers
- Don't allow credentials with wildcard origins
- Set appropriate `Access-Control-Max-Age`

## Secrets Management
- Store secrets in environment variables or a secrets manager — NEVER in code
- Don't log secrets, tokens, or passwords
- Use different secrets for each environment (dev, staging, production)
- Rotate secrets regularly
- Use constant-time comparison for token validation
- Scan commits for accidentally committed secrets (use pre-commit hooks)

## Audit Logging
- Log all authentication events (login, logout, failed attempts)
- Log all authorization failures
- Log data modifications (who changed what, when)
- Log admin actions
- Don't log sensitive data (passwords, tokens, PII) in plain text
- Store audit logs separately from application logs
- Ensure logs are tamper-resistant
- Include: timestamp, user_id, action, resource, result, source_ip

## HTTP Security Headers
Set these headers on all responses:
- `Content-Security-Policy` — restrict resource loading
- `X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff` — prevent MIME sniffing
- `X-Frame-Options: DENY` — prevent clickjacking
- `Strict-Transport-Security` — enforce HTTPS
- `Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin` — limit referrer info
- `Permissions-Policy` — restrict browser features

## Data Protection
- Classify data by sensitivity (public, internal, confidential, restricted)
- Apply appropriate protection for each classification
- Minimize data collection — only collect what you need
- Implement data retention policies
- Handle PII according to applicable regulations (GDPR, CCPA, etc.)
- Provide data export and deletion capabilities for user data
- Encrypt sensitive fields at the application level where needed

## Supply Chain Security
- Pin all dependency versions exactly — no ranges
- Use lockfiles and verify integrity hashes
- Audit dependencies for vulnerabilities on every CI run
- Review new dependencies before adding (maintainer reputation, license, popularity)
- Monitor for dependency deprecation and EOL announcements
- Consider SBOM (Software Bill of Materials) generation for production deployments
